The federal legal texts I have access to do not cover cantonal law, so I cannot state the specific additional integration criteria required by the Canton of Zurich.

Under Swiss federal law, the cantons are explicitly permitted to establish additional integration criteria beyond the federal baseline [1], as well as to regulate the cantonal and communal naturalisation procedure [2] and set a minimum cantonal and communal residence requirement of between two and five years [3].

At the federal level, ordinary naturalisation requires that an applicant is successfully integrated, is familiar with the Swiss way of life, and does not pose a threat to Switzerland's internal or external security [4]. Federal law defines successful integration in particular through:
- respecting public security and order [5];
- respecting the values of the Federal Constitution [5];
- being able to communicate in everyday life in speech and writing in a national language [5];
- participating in economic life or acquiring an education [5];
- encouraging and supporting the integration of one's spouse, registered partner, or minor children over whom parental custody is exercised [5].

Personal circumstances such as disability, illness, or other weighty personal factors that make meeting the language or economic/educational criteria impossible or difficult must be taken into account appropriately [6].

